# NULL pointer dereference in amdgpu_hmm_invalidate_gfx() (unguarded bo-\>vm_bo) — gfx1100 / RX 7900 XTX

## Summary

`amdgpu_hmm_invalidate_gfx()` dereferences `bo->vm_bo` unconditionally in its variable initializer. When the notifier fires for a BO that is not mapped into a GPU VM (`bo->vm_bo == NULL`), the kernel takes a NULL pointer dereference (read at `0x0`) inside the mmu-interval-notifier invalidate callback, i.e. in atomic/notifier context. The faulting access is `bo->vm_bo->vm`.

The crash is deterministic for any BO that reaches the callback in that state. On my machine it triggered 4 times over \~15.5 h before the accumulated damage (held `notifier_lock`, leaked refcounts) cascaded into an unrecoverable desktop freeze and forced power-off (which in turn corrupted the root filesystem).

## Offending code (current `master`, `drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_hmm.c`)

```c
static bool amdgpu_hmm_invalidate_gfx(struct mmu_interval_notifier *mni,
				      const struct mmu_notifier_range *range,
				      unsigned long cur_seq)
{
	struct amdgpu_bo *bo = container_of(mni, struct amdgpu_bo, notifier);
	struct amdgpu_device *adev = amdgpu_ttm_adev(bo->tbo.bdev);
	struct amdgpu_bo *vm_root = bo->vm_bo->vm->root.bo;   /* <-- bo->vm_bo may be NULL */
	long r;

	if (!mmu_notifier_range_blockable(range))
		return false;

	mutex_lock(&adev->notifier_lock);
	mmu_interval_set_seq(mni, cur_seq);
	amdgpu_vm_bo_invalidate(bo, false);
	r = dma_resv_wait_timeout(vm_root->tbo.base.resv

In AMD adrenaline the memory timings can be tuned to either “default” or “fast”:

![IMG_9842.jpeg](/uploads/59a6d0770b752b0bd74fa07ea72ca45c/IMG_9842.jpeg){width=614 height=340}

I asked the kind maintainers of LACT (https://github.com/ilya-zlobintsev/LACT) if this feature could be implemented in their tool, however they pointed out to me that this feature is lacking at the driver level, and thus this issue is about adding this functionality.
